    "https://guilford.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Guilford_Careers/job/US-NC-Greensboro/Electrician-Electronics-Technician_JR1429": {
      "title": "Electrician/Electronics Technician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPeople are drawn to Guilford College for a number of reasons, including the College’s   Core Values   of community, diversity, equality, excellence, integrity, justice, and stewardship. Working at Guilford provides you with a rewarding opportunity to impact the developing lives of  students from a range of diverse backgrounds . Guilford attracts those who are seeking a professional challenge and career advancement. It also attracts those who want to enjoy a beautiful campus and location in the dynamic city of  Greensboro, North Carolina . Guilford College offers a wide range of benefits for full-time regular employees, including 13 paid holidays, tuition remission, health insurance, telemedicine, life insurance, short-term and long-term disability, a retirement plan, and optional dental and vision insurance. In addition, employment at the College provides employees with access to numerous discounts for products, services, and attractions. Position Summary Basic Function Performs at a level of a certified electrical/electronics technician. Installs, maintains, repairs and troubleshoots electrical devices, wiring, equipment and incoming services including single to three phase components. Will also be required to perform maintenance duties in other related areas of plumbing, carpentry and general services. Will be in an on call status with other personnel. Duties and Responsibilities Ability to work alone and know when to schedule assistance. Electronic troubleshooting Wiring and conduit sizing, grounding, proper safety practice per national electric code. Use of voltmeters, Ohmmeters, circuit finders and millivolts to troubleshoot and locate electrical problems to make corrections. Read blueprints, make recommendations for electrical systems and to assist in layout planning to ensure the systems are operating at an efficient energy saving level. Have ability to lift 50 pounds, use ladders, hot and cold areas, crawl spaces and roof surfaces. Includes general maintenance on assigned golf cart, maintain tool safety and responsibility. Ability to stoop, stand, crawl, lift, listen, plan, schedule and organize to maintain timely efficiency. Maintain daily work sheet of time that reflects work orders, maintain timely work order completion, and maintain monthly time sheets. May require overtime. Experience Two years experience in a trade school or equivalent experience at job training Must be certified electrician in North Carolina Knowledge of switchgear, transfer switches, phases, wiring schematics, electronic troubleshooting Wiring, proper safety practice per national electric code Proven use of voltmeters, Ohmmeters, circuit finders and millivolts. Experience to see a connection with chillers and boilers from an operational view in troubleshooting and to maintain operation with no down time. Guilford College does not discriminate on the basis of sex/gender, race, color, creed, religion, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, genetic information, military status, veteran status, or any other protected category under applicable local, state or federal law, ordinance or regulation. See our   full Statement of Non-Discrimination ."
    },
    "https://guilford.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Guilford_Careers/job/US-NC-Greensboro/Plumber---Certified-Boiler-Technician_JR1430": {
      "title": "Plumber & Certified Boiler Technician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPeople are drawn to Guilford College for a number of reasons, including the College’s   Core Values   of community, diversity, equality, excellence, integrity, justice, and stewardship. Working at Guilford provides you with a rewarding opportunity to impact the developing lives of  students from a range of diverse backgrounds . Guilford attracts those who are seeking a professional challenge and career advancement. It also attracts those who want to enjoy a beautiful campus and location in the dynamic city of  Greensboro, North Carolina . Guilford College offers a wide range of benefits for full-time regular employees, including 13 paid holidays, tuition remission, health insurance, telemedicine, life insurance, short-term and long-term disability, a retirement plan, and optional dental and vision insurance. In addition, employment at the College provides employees with access to numerous discounts for products, services, and attractions. Position Summary Basic Function Contributes to the effective operation of the Physical Plant. Responsible for the installation and maintenance of plumbing, piping and other equipment at all buildings owned and operated by the College. These duties include work of plumbing tradesperson’s competence, physical capability, and mechanical and troubleshooting aptitude. This position also requires certification in boilers. Duties & Responsibilities Performs a broad range of plumbing and maintenance services of the Physical Plant within the Facilities Division: Installs, maintains, trouble shoots, tests, and repairs plant components, some of which include: steam and hot water heating system, heating systems components including pneumatic and electrical valves, compressors, boilers, circulators, septic systems, roof drains, fire system devices, rain water leaders, sewage lift pump stations, domestic water systems, water treatment systems, sinks, piping, building controls, fountains, kitchen equipment, drains, various types of plumbing fixtures, along with other plumbing and mechanical machinery, plant and utility systems; Carries out emergency, planned and preventative maintenance on all assigned equipment within the College. Responds to emergency calls that are a result of mechanical/plumbing failure both during and after regular hours of work; Responsible to assume work duties on plant systems and equipment, or perform various tasks normally designated to other department members when required; Ensures that all work is done according to standard trade practices, US Plumbing Code, and provincial and local building codes; Keeps appropriate records including complete equipment repair history, work orders, daily job card, vehicle records, test reports, and other related data; Determines sufficient material inventory. Locates correct replacement parts and suppliers. Initiates ordering of supplies and replacement parts Liaisons with other consultants, contractors, inspectors, suppliers, and other departments on maintenance and project work. Assists other College staff and external service providers; Accurately completes and documents work orders prior to submission to supervisor. Provides written reports on any inspections and/or tests and makes recommendations to improve performance; Maintains clean and orderly conditions in all working areas, including shop, vehicle and school sites; Ensures duties are performed in a fiscally responsible manner while observing all safety practices and regulations; Performs any other duties as required. Competencies Required The Plumber, shall have the following competencies The ability to work and communicate effectively within a team environment; The ability to support and communicate effectively with third parties including students, school staff, parents, and outside entities; The ability to organize and employ writing skills of the nature required to effectively communicate with the Director and external service providers; The ability to prioritize in the scheduling of work requirements; The ability to maintain a professional appearance and attitude on the job The ability to work within a team or individually, as required, with minimal supervision; An understanding of, and ability to carry out, policies and procedures developed by the Facilities Department; The ability to read and interpret mechanical drawings, schematics, blueprints, plumbing code specifications, and diagrams for installation and repair of building systems and equipment; A working knowledge of building systems, including mechanical and electrical; A knowledge of applicable regulatory, building and trades guidelines and codes; A working knowledge of the various types of plumbing, space heating, utility controls and equipment, fire suppression systems, and water treatment; The ability to work with a computer as a programming and troubleshooting tool; The desire and ability to engage in continuing education and skills upgrading; The ability to understand and promote confidentiality as the norm; The ability to apply knowledge, experience and commitment in the areas of race relations, cross cultural understanding and human rights; Knowledge of Health & Safety procedures, which include fire and emergency plans, and Department of Labor procedures. Qualifications High School Diploma; Certification of Qualification as a Plumber recognized by North Carolina; Certified Boiler Technician Two years experience in the plumbing trade and/or minimum 2 years in trade school or a building maintenance environment; Additional technical experience or qualified certification in areas such as Steamfitter/Pipefitter, Welding, HVAC, Electrical, and Carpentry; Valid North Carolina driver’s license and excellent driving record; Physical and mechanical aptitude to perform a full range of maintenance and manual task. Work Environment (Equipment, Demands, Hazards): Ability and dexterity in use of various manual and powered hand tools; Must be able to use various equipment such as powered snakes, ladders, staging, and man lifts; Work involved with pressured hydronic systems, mechanical and boiler rooms, ventilation rooms, small spaces, and moving equipment; Occasional periods of high levels of physical exertion are needed. This includes heavy lifting, climbing, crouching, balancing, reaching, stooping, carrying, working in confined spaces, above or below normal temperatures, etc; Working at times with very small parts, in areas of public access, and individually and with others; Provide own hand tools and required to travel between various sites within College; Normal work period is spent indoors with occasional outdoor periods; Work evenings, days, and weekends as required. Hours of work and terms of employment subject to Collective Agreement, including callback and overtime, eight or ten hour shifts. Guilford College does not discriminate on the basis of sex/gender, race, color, creed, religion, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, genetic information, military status, veteran status, or any other protected category under applicable local, state or federal law, ordinance or regulation. See our   full Statement of Non-Discrimination ."
    },
    "https://guilford.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Guilford_Careers/job/US-NC-Greensboro/Part-Time-Choir-Director_JR1340": {
      "title": "Part-Time Choir Director",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-26\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPeople are drawn to Guilford College for a number of reasons, including the College’s   Core Values   of community, diversity, equality, excellence, integrity, justice, and stewardship. Working at Guilford provides you with a rewarding opportunity to impact the developing lives of  students from a range of diverse backgrounds . Guilford attracts those who are seeking a professional challenge and career advancement. It also attracts those who want to enjoy a beautiful campus and location in the dynamic city of  Greensboro, North Carolina . Guilford College offers a wide range of benefits for full-time regular employees, including 13 paid holidays, tuition remission, health insurance, telemedicine, life insurance, short-term and long-term disability, a retirement plan, and optional dental and vision insurance. In addition, employment at the College provides employees with access to numerous discounts for products, services, and attractions. Position Summary Guilford College is seeking a Director of the College Choir for the 2027-28 Academic year, with employment beginning in January for the Spring 2027 semester. This appointment is a part-time instructor position. The director will have a firm foundation in classical training as well as experience with a diversity of repertoire in the field of choral music. The director will organize and lead the College Choir which rehearses twice weekly and performs a concert at the end of both the Fall and Spring semesters, as well as at the spring commencement ceremony. The ensemble traditionally includes both college music majors and non-majors, as well as high school students from the Early College at Guilford. The ideal candidate will have experience working with vocalists of a range of ages and abilities , while taking an active role working with the student Choir Officers in recruiting choir members from the campus community. We seek a dynamic and vibrant artist to lead our diverse group of musicians and continue the long tradition of the Guilford College Choir. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Direct the Guilford College Choir (75 minute rehearsals, twice weekly) Participate in choir recruitment efforts as appropriate for a part-time position. Produce a fall concert in collaboration with the other music department ensembles (December). Produce a spring concert (April). Preferred Qualifications A master’s degree in music preferred with an emphasis in choral conducting or voice, or significant professional experience. Teaching experience at the collegiate level Experience with a diversity of choral repertoire  Guilford College does not discriminate on the basis of sex/gender, race, color, creed, religion, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, genetic information, military status, veteran status, or any other protected category under applicable local, state or federal law, ordinance or regulation. See our   full Statement of Non-Discrimination ."
    },
    "https://guilford.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Guilford_Careers/job/US-NC-Greensboro/Assistant-Men-s-and-Women-s-Tennis-Coach_JR1385": {
      "title": "Assistant Men's and Women's Tennis Coach",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-10\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPeople are drawn to Guilford College for a number of reasons, including the College’s   Core Values   of community, diversity, equality, excellence, integrity, justice, and stewardship. Working at Guilford provides you with a rewarding opportunity to impact the developing lives of  students from a range of diverse backgrounds . Guilford attracts those who are seeking a professional challenge and career advancement. It also attracts those who want to enjoy a beautiful campus and location in the dynamic city of  Greensboro, North Carolina . Guilford College offers a wide range of benefits for full-time regular employees, including 13 paid holidays, tuition remission, health insurance, telemedicine, life insurance, short-term and long-term disability, a retirement plan, and optional dental and vision insurance. In addition, employment at the College provides employees with access to numerous discounts for products, services, and attractions. Position Summary Guilford College invites applications for the position of Assistant Men's & Women’s Tennis Coach. This is a part-time position responsible for assisting in the overall operation and coaching of the tennis programs in accordance with NCAA Division III, Old Dominion Athletic Conference (ODAC), and Guilford College guidelines. Primary Responsibilities: Assist the Head Coaches with organizing and conducting practice sessions.   Support student-athlete skill development on and off the court.   Assist in recruiting responsibilities.   Uphold the academic and athletic mission of Guilford College.   Adhere to all college, conference, and NCAA policies.   Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree preferred.   Collegiate-level playing and/or coaching experience strongly preferred.   Knowledge of NCAA Division III rules and regulations.   Strong interpersonal, communication, and organizational skills.   Valid driver’s license and ability to travel with the team.   CPR/AED certification (or willingness to obtain upon hire).   Desired Attributes: A passion for developing young athletes both personally and athletically.   Ability to foster a culture of inclusion, sportsmanship, and academic excellence.   Commitment to the Division III philosophy and the overall student-athlete experience Guilford College does not discriminate on the basis of sex/gender, race, color, creed, religion, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, genetic information, military status, veteran status, or any other protected category under applicable local, state or federal law, ordinance or regulation. See our   full Statement of Non-Discrimination ."
    },
    "https://guilford.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Guilford_Careers/job/US-NC-Greensboro/Project-Manager-Be-the-Example_JR1349": {
      "title": "Project Director - Be the Example",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-04\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPeople are drawn to Guilford College for a number of reasons, including the College’s   Core Values   of community, diversity, equality, excellence, integrity, justice, and stewardship. Working at Guilford provides you with a rewarding opportunity to impact the developing lives of  students from a range of diverse backgrounds . Guilford attracts those who are seeking a professional challenge and career advancement. It also attracts those who want to enjoy a beautiful campus and location in the dynamic city of  Greensboro, North Carolina . Guilford College offers a wide range of benefits for full-time regular employees, including 13 paid holidays, tuition remission, health insurance, telemedicine, life insurance, short-term and long-term disability, a retirement plan, and optional dental and vision insurance. In addition, employment at the College provides employees with access to numerous discounts for products, services, and attractions. Position Summary Revised 8/4/2026 Time-limited (Grant-funded) . Continued employment is highly anticipated based on performance and the successful submission of the competitive grant renewal. Term: 13-month initial contract ( Current cycle end date: September 30, 2027 ). SUMMARY:  The Project Director serves as the Institutional Strategist and Compliance Officer for the College’s Office on Violence Against Women (OVW) Campus Grant. This specialized role focuses on the high-level administrative, fiscal, and logistical management of the grant, ensuring total compliance with federal mandates and seamless communication with the federal OVW Grant Manager. Rather than executing day-to-day event planning, the Project Director designs and oversees a structured, \"staff-led, peer-executed\" prevention model. The Director provides rigorous training, trauma-informed guardrails, and programmatic oversight to a select student peer-education cohort tasked with delivering campus-wide awareness campaigns. Additionally, the Director chairs the Coordinated Community Response Team (CCRT), leads institutional policy revisions, and acts as the principal author for the grant's competitive renewal and future funding cycles. This position reports to the Director of Counseling, with executive oversight and strategic guidance provided by the Assistant Provost, the Chief Dean of Students, and the campus grant management team. ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES:  The following statements reflect the general responsibilities of the position and should not be construed as a detailed description of all the work requirements that may be inherent in this position. To perform this job successfully, an individual must perform each essential duty satisfactorily. 1. Grant Management & Federal Compliance (40%) Serves as the primary system user for JustGrants; collaborates directly with the Grants Accountant to manage fiscal drawdowns, monitor expenditures, and maintain rigorous budget oversight. Manages the comprehensive grant budget, tracking expenditures, ensuring fiscal accountability, and processing timely budget modifications in alignment with federal and College regulations. Compiles, verifies, and submits all required federal documentations, including semi-annual progress reports and Financial Status Reports (FFRs), maintaining clean communication and strong rapport with the federal OVW Grant Manager. Monitors the execution of the approved OVW Strategic Plan, tracking timeline deliverables and managing formal programmatic amendments. Attends mandatory OVW Technical Assistance (TA) conferences and training institutes as required by the federal grant manager (requires occasional travel, approximately 5-10%). 2. Strategic Oversight of Peer Education (25%) Recruits, trains, and mentors a designated student peer-education group tasked with delivering non-confidential, public-facing campus awareness and prevention programming (e.g., bystander intervention, tabling, awareness months). Establishes strict programmatic and legal guardrails for the student cohort, vetting all presentation materials to ensure alignment with OVW compliance, Clery Act definitions, and Title IX reporting mandates. Provides trauma-informed oversight during student-facilitated workshops, serving as the professional on-call safety net to handle disclosure management, crisis containment, and immediate warm handoffs to confidential advocates. Acts as the primary institutional liaison and advocate for the student peer-education cohort; navigates campus bureaucracy to clear administrative hurdles, secure physical spaces, and negotiate collaborative programming partnerships with student affairs, residential life, and athletics, allowing students to focus strictly on peer-to-peer delivery. Aggregates and maintains precise data tracking systems for student-led metrics, including attendance logs and campus completion records for mandatory prevention programs. 3. Sustainability & Grantwriting (20%) Serves as the principal investigator/primary author for the competitive renewal of the OVW Campus Grant for the next funding cycle to secure programmatic continuity past September 2027. Identifies, drafts, and submits supplemental federal, state, or private grant applications to ensure the long-term sustainability of the campus’s Domestic Violence, Dating Violence, Sexual Assault, and Stalking (DVDVSAS) response infrastructure. Develops data-driven narratives and sustainability plans based on the measurable outcomes achieved during the current grant cycle. 4. Coordinated Community Response, Policy, & Institutional Integration (15%) Convenes, coordinates, and chairs the campus Coordinated Community Response Team (CCRT), ensuring robust participation from institutional leadership, faculty, students, public safety, and local community-based victim service agencies. Leads the CCRT in evaluating current campus policies and disciplinary procedures regarding DVDVSAS, delivering strategic recommendations to College executive leadership. Coordinates specialized, high-level training for institutional personnel involved in disciplinary, investigative, and public safety activities, ensuring compliance with evolving federal guidelines. Serves as the designated professional bridge between academic affairs, student conduct, campus public safety, and student health services; actively aligns disparate departmental policies to ensure a seamless, trauma-informed response network. Leverages professional staff status to build formal, high-level memorandum of understandings (MOUs) and workflows across campus units (e.g., Athletics, Student Organizations, Orientation Teams, and Human Resources) to structurally integrate education into mandatory institutional timelines. Provides specialized technical assistance and trauma-informed guidance to the Student Conduct office regarding the adjudication of non-Title IX domestic violence, dating violence, sexual assault, and stalking cases; assists in training standard conduct hearing officers to ensure institutional responses remain compliant with best practices. QUALIFICATIONS: The requirements listed below represent the knowledge, skill, and/or ability required. Some evening and weekend hours as needed and will include occasional travel for required training sessions. Education and Experience: Required: Bachelor’s degree in Higher Education Administration, Public Administration, Criminal Justice, or a related field with 5+ years of relevant grant-management experience. Preferred: Master of Public Health (MPH), Master of Social Work (MSW), or Master of Criminal Justice (MCJ). The structural design of this position is highly suited for macro-practice social workers, public health strategists, or criminal justice professionals specializing in institutional policy, federal compliance, and victim-centered systemic responses. Experience: Minimum of three (3) years of professional experience working within a higher education ecosystem or an established victim-services agency. A combination of education and experience can be considered. Demonstrated experience navigating federal grant portals (JustGrants, Grant Solutions, or SAM.gov) and managing complex, multi-dimensional project budgets. Proven track record of successful grant writing, proposal development, or program sustainability planning. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Subject Matter Expertise: Deep professional knowledge of DVDVSAS dynamics, trauma-informed care practices, Title IX regulations, and Clery Act compliance. Leadership & Group Facilitation: Ability to provide clinical/professional boundaries and mentorship to undergraduate or graduate student cohorts, mitigating risks associated with secondary traumatic stress. Strategic Communication: Exceptional technical writing skills for federal reporting and grant design; strong administrative diplomacy required to interface between federal oversight, college executives, law enforcement, and community partners. Technical Proficiency: Advanced working knowledge of Google Suite, project management platforms, and financial tracking systems. Guilford College does not discriminate on the basis of sex/gender, race, color, creed, religion, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, genetic information, military status, veteran status, or any other protected category under applicable local, state or federal law, ordinance or regulation. See our   full Statement of Non-Discrimination ."
    },
